## Proof

Let $M$ be a matrix representing $f$ in some basis, so $M$ is $n\times n$ over $K$. Consider the **characteristic matrix**

$$XI - M \in M_n(K[X]).$$

This is a matrix over the PID $K[X]$. Its Smith normal form is diagonal with entries $q_1, q_2, \ldots, q_n$ (the similarity invariants of $f$), satisfying the divisibility chain

$$q_1 \mid q_2 \mid \cdots \mid q_n.$$

This is in fact one standard *definition* of the similarity invariants: they are the invariant factors of the characteristic matrix $XI - M$.

---

### Determinantal divisors

For $k = 1, \ldots, n$, define the **$k$-th determinantal divisor**

$$D_k(X) = \gcd\{\text{all } k\times k \text{ minors of } XI - M\},$$

with the convention $D_0 = 1$.

**Key fact (Smith normal form).** The $k\times k$ minors of a matrix and of its Smith normal form generate the same ideal in $K[X]$, since passing to Smith normal form only uses invertible row/column operations over $K[X]$, which preserve the gcd of $k\times k$ minors.

For the Smith normal form $\operatorname{diag}(q_1,\ldots,q_n)$, every $k\times k$ minor is (up to sign) a product $q_{i_1}\cdots q_{i_k}$ with $1\le i_1<\cdots<i_k\le n$, or $0$. Using the divisibility chain $q_1\mid\cdots\mid q_n$, the gcd of all such products is

$$D_k = q_1\,q_2\,\cdots\,q_k,$$

since $q_1\cdots q_k$ divides every product $q_{i_1}\cdots q_{i_k}$ (each $q_{i_j}$ is a multiple of $q_j$), and $q_1\cdots q_k$ itself occurs as the minor on rows/columns $1,\ldots,k$.

---

### The relationship

Combining the two observations:

$$\boxed{\gcd\{\text{all } k\times k \text{ minors of } XI - M\} \;=\; q_1\,q_2\,\cdots\,q_k.}$$

Equivalently, dividing consecutive determinantal divisors recovers the similarity invariants:

$$q_k = \frac{D_k}{D_{k-1}} = \frac{\gcd\{k\times k\text{ minors of }XI-M\}}{\gcd\{(k-1)\times(k-1)\text{ minors of }XI-M\}}, \qquad k=1,\ldots,n,$$

with $D_0 = 1$.

---

### Independence of the representing matrix

If $M'$ is another matrix of $f$ in a different basis, then $M' = PMP^{-1}$ for some $P\in\mathrm{GL}_n(K)$. Since $P$ and $P^{-1}$ are invertible over $K\subset K[X]$,

$$XI - M' = P(XI - M)P^{-1},$$

so $XI-M'$ and $XI-M$ are equivalent over $K[X]$. Hence they have the same Smith normal form, the same invariant factors, and the same determinantal divisors. The relationship above is therefore independent of the chosen basis, as it must be since the $q_k$ are invariants of $f$ itself.

---

### Summary

For each $k = 1,\ldots, n$:

- The **greatest common divisor** of all $k\times k$ minors of $XI - M$ equals the product $q_1 q_2 \cdots q_k$ of the first $k$ similarity invariants.
- Consequently $q_k = D_k/D_{k-1}$, i.e. the $k$-th similarity invariant is the ratio of the gcd of $k\times k$ minors to the gcd of $(k-1)\times(k-1)$ minors of $XI - M$.

